{{citation needed|Kitaguchi Hongū Fuji Sengen Shrine}}

'''Kitaguchi Hongū Fuji Sengen Shrine''' (北口本宮冨士浅間神社, Kitaguchi Hongū Fuji Sengen Jinja) is a {{ill|Shinto shrine|ja|神社|12=draft}} located in Kamiyoshida, {{ill|Fujiyoshida|ja|富士吉田市|12=draft}}, {{ill|Yamanashi Prefecture|ja|山梨県|12=draft}}. Its former Rank under the {{ill|Modern system of Ranked Shinto shrines|ja|近代Rank制度|12=draft}} was that of a Prefectural Shrine, and it is now designated as a Beppyo Shrine under the jurisdiction of the {{ill|Association of Shinto Shrines|ja|神社本庁|12=draft}}. It serves as the starting point for the Yoshida Trail of {{ill|Mount Fuji|ja|富士山|12=draft}}, and is part of the "Mount Fuji" component of the World Cultural Heritage Site "Fujisan, sacred place and source of artistic inspiration."{{citation needed|Kitaguchi Hongū Fuji Sengen Shrine}}

{{citation needed|Kitaguchi Hongū Fuji Sengen Shrine}}

{{citation needed|Kitaguchi Hongū Fuji Sengen Shrine}}

{{citation needed|Kitaguchi Hongū Fuji Sengen Shrine}}

{{citation needed|Kitaguchi Hongū Fuji Sengen Shrine}}

==deity==
* {{ill|Konohanasakuya-hime|ja|木花開耶姫命|1=Konohanasakuya-hime|12=draft}} (Konohanasakuya-hime no Mikoto)
* Heavenly grandson {{ill|Ninigi|ja|彦火瓊瓊杵命|12=draft}} (Hikohono Ninigi no Mikoto) - Husband deity
* {{ill|Ōyamatsumi|ja|大山祇神|12=draft}} (Ōyamatsumi) - Father deity

==Major festivals==
* New Year festival - February 17
* Hatsusumij-sai (festival) - May 5
* Chinka-sai (Yoshida Fire festival) - August 26, 27
* Niinamesai - November 23

==History==
In the 40th year of Emperor Keikō, it is said that during his eastward expedition, {{ill|Yamato Takeru|ja|日本武尊|1=Yamato Takeru|lt=Prince Yamato Takeru|3=ヤマトタケル|12=draft}} passed through the area, where he enshrined a deity and built a small shrine at the command to ascend Mount Fuji from the north side.

In the 7th year of Enryaku (788), the Governor of Kai Province, {{ill|Ki no Toyonawa|ja|紀豊庭|12=draft}}, constructed the current shrine and enshrined the great deity of Asama, along with the spirit of Yamato Takeru at Ōtsukaoka. The shrine has undergone various reconstructions and renovations over the centuries, with the current main hall built in Genwa 1 (1615) by the Lord of {{ill|Tanimura Castle|ja|谷村城|12=draft}}, Tosa-no-Kami Torii Seiji. The shrine buildings were renovated again in Jōkyō 5 (1688).

In Kyōhō 18 (1733), {{ill|Muraoka Mitsukiyo|ja|村上光清|12=draft}}, the leader of the {{ill|Fuji cult|ja|富士講|12=draft}}, financed and built the Heiden, Haiden, Kaguraden, Chōzuya, and Zuishinmon.

The shrine is located at the starting point of the Yoshida Trail of Mount Fuji, and during the Edo period, it became the center of the Fuji cult. At its peak, the area had nearly 100 {{ill|shukubō|ja|宿坊|12=draft}} (lodging houses for pilgrims). In the early Shōwa period, a bus route was operated from the back of the shrine to the Mount Fuji climbing route.

During the Edo period, the name "Asama Ōbōsatsu" became more prominent than "Suwa Daimyōjin." In the Meiji period, the shrine was renamed "Fujisan Kitaguchi Hongū Fujidake Shrine," and later simply "Sengen Shrine." In 1946, it was renamed to its current name, "Kitaguchi Hongū Fuji Sengen Shrine."

The shrine's connection to {{ill|Shinano no Okami|ja|信濃大明神|12=draft}} was established when {{ill|Shinano no Okami|ja|信濃大明神|12=draft}} (Suwa Myōjin) was merged into Kitaguchi Hongū as a subsidiary shrine.

